<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chan Chan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 850–1470</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chan Chan was the capital of the Chimú kingdom on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, near modern Trujillo, and the largest adobe city of the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. It contained some ten walled royal compounds with courts, storerooms and burial platforms decorated with relief friezes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered it around 1470, and it is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chan-chan.jpg" alt="Chan Chan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Håkan Svensson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chan_chan_view1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
